+On the 1st of July, Jenner did the test. He gave James a standard  variolation, the old treatment that used real smallpox  matter, which would normally cause at least a mild illness.  Then he waited. One day. Three days. A week. Nothing happened.  In Jenner's words, no disease followed. Months later he tried again. Still  nothing. The cowpox had protected the boy.  Jenner wasn't the first to try cowpox. Benjamin Jesty had done  it 22 years earlier. What Jenner did that was new was test it  carefully and then publish every case so any doctor could check  his work and do it themselves. He tested more people and in 1798  he paid to print a short book. He called the disease by a Latin name  varioli vaccinae, which means smallpox of the cow. From  Vaca, the Latin word for cow, came the name for the treatment  vaccination. Not everyone believed him. Some doctors mocked  the idea and cartoonists drew people sprouting little cows  heads after their jab, but the results were hard to argue  with. Jenner sent cowpox matter to anyone who asked and in a  little hut in his own garden in Barkley, he vaccinated poor  families for free. Within a few years, doctors were  vaccinating people across Britain, Europe and the  Americas.
